#---
# purpose: "a construction whose capture type is a union is admitted only by a test that CONTAINS it, never one it merely overlaps"
# oracle: oracle.exs
#---
#
# fz-kdt.167, the witness. `f` is chosen by a `case` on a value that came out
# of the mailbox, so no key can pin which closure it is: one arm mints `mk/1`
# over that mailbox value, whose capture is typed `any` and stored as a BOXED
# word, and the other mints it over a `float` literal, stored raw. `P.run/2`
# takes the union of the two, and a runtime test on the construction is the
# only thing that can separate them.
#
# The `any` construction OVERLAPS the float arm -- an `any` capture could be
# holding a float -- but it is INSIDE neither arm, because the layout a capture
# was stored in belongs to the construction and not to the value that happens
# to sit there. Admitting on overlap therefore hands a boxed capture to a body
# whose capture lane is a raw f64: the interpreter answers 12.0 instead of 12,
# and the native doors abort in `fz_closure_get_capture_f64`. Admission is
# containment.
#
# This fixture used to witness the same law with four STATIC forwarded calls.
# fz-kdt.127's type-preserving forwarder key answers that shape statically --
# `P.run/2` keys one activation per capture type, every callsite has one
# target, and the program lost its dispatch node entirely (52 questions -> 16,
# 1 dispatch node -> 0). The closure has to come out of a runtime branch for
# the question to survive the key (fz-kdt.171).
defmodule P do
  fn twice(f, x), do: f.(f.(x))
  fn run(f, x), do: twice(f, x)
  fn mk(c), do: fn (n) -> n + c end
end

fn main() do
  send(self(), 1)
  a = receive do v -> v end
  f = case a > 0 do
    true -> P.mk(a)
    _ -> P.mk(0.5)
  end
  dbg(P.run(f, 10))
end
